==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈfaɪɹɪŋ/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-firing.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪəɹɪŋ}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|ceramics}} The process of applying [[heat]] or [[fire]], especially to [[clay]] etc to produce [[pottery]].
#: ''After the pots have been glazed, they go back into the kiln for a second '''firing'''.''
# The [[fuel]] for a fire.
#* {{circa|1611}}, {{w|William Shakespeare}}, ''{{w|The Tempest}}'', Act 2, Scene 2,<sup>[http://www.opensourceshakespeare.org/views/plays/play_view.php?WorkID=tempest&Scope=entire&pleasewait=1&msg=pl]</sup>
#*: No more dams I’ll make for fish;
#*: Nor fetch in '''firing'''
#*: At requiring {{...}}
#* {{RQ:Orwell Down and Out|chapter=25|passage=Downstairs there was a kitchen common to all lodgers, with free '''firing''' and a supply of cooking-pots, tea-basins, and toasting-forks.}}
# The act of adding fuel to a fire.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1961|month=February|author=""Balmore""|title=Driving and firing modern French steam locomotives - Part One|journal=Trains Illustrated|page=109|text=The doors are at the right level for '''firing''', which normally is down one side of the firebox at a time, unlike our own practice, which is to fire each side of the firebox with alternate shovelfuls.}}
# The [[discharge]] of a [[gun]] or other [[weapon]].
#* '''1719''', {{w|Daniel Defoe}}, ''{{w|Robinson Crusoe}}'', London: W. Taylor, p. 308,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004845034.0001.000]</sup>
#*: {{...}} they fir’d several Times, making other Signals for the Boat.
#*: At last, when all their Signals and '''Firings''' prov’d fruitless, and they found the Boat did not stir, we saw them by the Help of my Glasses, hoist another Boat out, and row towards the Shore {{...}}
#* '''1940''', {{w|Ernest Hemingway}}, ''{{w|For Whom the Bell Tolls}}'', London: Jonathan Cape, Chapter 43, p. 417,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.463378]</sup>
#*: He heard the '''firing''' and as he walked he felt it in the pit of his stomach as though it echoed on his own diaphragm.
# The [[dismissal]] of someone from a [[job]].
#* '''2016''', {{w|Matthew d'Ancona|Matthew d’Ancona}}, “Theresa May’s Shock Therapy,” ''{{w|The New York Times}}'', 19 July, 2016,<sup>[https://www.nytimes.com/2016/07/20/opinion/theresa-mays-shock-therapy.html]</sup>
#*: Even the most seasoned analysts of British politics were struck by the brutality of Ms. May’s hirings and '''firings'''.
# [[cauterization|Cauterization]].

